  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain with isolated thunderstorms through \ntonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nRain showers will continue through this evening. With plenty of\navailable moisture to work with, moderate rainfall is likely\nwithin the heavier showers. A cold front will push through later\ntonight, potentially bringing an extra gust of winds along with\nanother chance for lightning along the front. Wind gusts tonight\nthrough the day tomorrow may peak around 25 to 30 mph. Continued\nwinds will lessen any chance for fog development, though\nsheltered valleys could still see some fog early Tuesday and\nWednesday mornings.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Scattered showers and thunderstorms late this \nweek. Some severe storms are possible. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure system approaches from the northwest, moving into\nNew Brunswick. Associated cold front swings through on Friday,\nthrough Maine by Saturday morning. There is some instability \nahead of the cold front. GFS is currently showing around 1,500 \nJ/kg of CAPE, good low level lapse rates (6-7C/km in the north,\nand 8-9C/km Downeast), and plenty of precipitation. Soundings \nare moist, with classic long and skinny CAPE. PWAT values around\n1.8\u201d possible, which is around the 90th percentile for Caribou,\napproaching the 1.9\u201d daytime maximum value. Continue to monitor\nfor updates, as the regional and CAMs gradually begin to pick \nup on the system.",
